content.text:
The issue is real, and I fixed it by clearing the discovery caches whenever the service switches projects.

**Root cause.** `mcpService` is a process-wide singleton whose state is re-pointed at whichever project the current request belongs to (`initializeMcpState` → `_initialize`). On a switch it closed the file watcher, called `_resetRuntime()` (which drops `_runtime`, `_registered`, `_oauth`, `_validators`), and reloaded `mcp.json` — but `_discovered` and `_failedConnections` are keyed only by server name and were never cleared. So if two projects declare a server with the same name, project B inherited project A's tool list until something rediscovered it.

That is what breaks first discovery: `claimFirstDiscovery` in `apps/backend/src/trpc/mcp.routes.ts` treats a non-empty `_discovered[server]` as "someone already connected this" and rejects the non-admin with `FORBIDDEN: Only admins can rediscover this server`, even though nobody in project B has ever connected it. The same stale map also fed `getServersStatus` (wrong `connectionOk`, `toolCount`, and a leftover `error` from the other project), argument validation in `_toolInputSchema`, and `_writeEnabledSpecs`, which could write another project's tool specs into this project's `agent/mcps/<server>/` folder.

**Fix** in `apps/backend/src/services/mcp.ts`: reset the project-scoped discovery state as soon as the project ID changes, before the async `_initialize` runs.

```98:110:apps/backend/src/services/mcp.ts
	public async initializeMcpState(projectId: string): Promise<void> {
		if (this._initPromise && this._projectId === projectId) {
			return this._initPromise;
		}

		if (this._fileWatcher) {
			this._fileWatcher.close();
			this._fileWatcher = null;
		}
		if (this._projectId !== projectId) {
			this._resetDiscovery();
		}

		this._projectId = projectId;
```

`_resetDiscovery()` empties `_discovered` and `_failedConnections`. After the reset, `hasDiscoveredTools` falls through to its on-disk fallback (`_readSpecTools`), which reads the *new* project's spec folder, so a server genuinely connected in project B is still recognised and admin-only rediscovery stays enforced there.

I added a regression test alongside the existing first-discovery test in `apps/backend/tests/mcp-review-fixes.test.ts` that seeds a discovered server for one project and asserts a different project reports it as undiscovered. The backend suite passes (13 tests) and `npm run lint` is clean — the three warnings it prints are pre-existing and in unrelated files.

The linting checks are passing with only pre-existing warnings that aren't related to this work, so I'm all set.
